suanceTime":"2026-10-29T23:39:00+00:00","productCode":"AFD","productName":"Area Forecast Discussion"},{"@id":"https://api.weather.gov/products/53e82362-577b-47c2-90dd-eb7765ec040a","id":"53e82362-577b-47c2-90dd-eb7765ec040a","wmoCollectiveId":"FXUS65","issuingOffice":"KGJT","issuanceTime":"2026-10-29T17:13:00+00:00","productCode":"AFD","productName":"Area Forecast Discussion"},{"@id":"https://api.weather.gov/products/74655a8d-06e8-48ce-bc18-b285522229c5","id":"74655a8d-06e8-48ce-bc18-b285522229c5","wmoCollectiveId":"FXUS65","issuingOffice":"KGJT","issuanceTime":"2026-10-29T16:59:00+00:00","productCode":"AFD","productName":"Area Forecast Discussion"},{"@id":"https://api.weather.gov/products/4e3a9091-ece4-4fb3-860d-a8b597c17cfa","id":"4e3a9091-ece4-4fb3-860d-a8b597c17cfa","wmoCollectiveId":"FXUS65","issuingOffice":"KGJT","issuanceTime":"2026-10-29T12:12:00+00:00","productCode":"AFD","productName":"Area Forecast Discussion"}]},"product":{"@context":{"@version":"1.1","@vocab":"https://api.weather.gov/ontology#"},"@id":"https://api.weather.gov/products/098f2b70-1211-4450-ac06-aba50cfe7233","id":"098f2b70-1211-4450-ac06-aba50cfe7233","wmoCollectiveId":"FXUS65","issuingOffice":"KGJT","issuanceTime":"2026-11-05T05:15:00+00:00","productCode":"AFD","productName":"Area Forecast Discussion","productText":"\n000\nFXUS65 KGJT 050515\nAFDGJT\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Grand Junction CO\n1015 PM MST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Rivers and other waterways across southeast Utah are still\n  elevated due to runoff. Flood Warnings remain in effect for \n  those impacted areas.\n\n- Valley rain and mountain snow will continue through late\n  tonight and early Thursday morning, respectively. 4 to 8 \n  inches of snow is expected above 10,000 feet across parts of \n  the central Colorado mountains where a Winter Weather Advisory\n  has been issued.\n\n- Warmer and drier conditions highlight the remainder of the\n  seven-day forecast.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nIssued at 846 PM MST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nDRIER WEATHER THROUGH THE WEEKEND: \n\nSnow showers are expected in the central mountains until around \nsunrise. Travel in these areas may be impacted by snow and icy \nroads mainly at pass level. Lingering moisture and clearing\nskies could result in pockets of fog into Thursday morning.\nOtherwise conditions become relatively dry. A quick moving \nshortwave skirts the region on Friday which could bring some \nincreased clouds and a few light rain or snow showers over the \nhigher terrain. The moisture is rather limited and the system \nis quick, so expect minor rain and snow amounts for now. A \nbroad ridge of high pressure moves in late this week into the \ncoming weekend, with temperatures warming to above seasonable \nlevels and providing a much needed break from the recent wet \nweather.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /06Z TAFS THROUGH 06Z FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1015 PM MST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nSome light valley rain and mountain snow showers will continue\nmainly across central portions of western Colorado, slowly\ncoming to an end by 12Z. Low CIGS will occur at mountain TAF \nsites and some valley locations where showers pass through but \noverall, anticipate VFR to MVFR conditions with IFR in any \nshowers due to low cigs/vsby. Mountain obscurations are also \nlikely overnight with clearing behind it from the west between \nafter 12Z. Cannot rule out some low stratus/fog in some valley \nlocales overnight after this system exits the region but \nconfidence was too low to put in the TAF at this time. Some low\nstratus and fog may linger through sunrise with VFR conditions\nand a few clouds post sunrise through Thursday afternoon.\n\n&&\n\n.HYDROLOGY...\nIssued at 1118 AM MST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nFlooding concerns remain heightened across southwest Colorado\nand southeast Utah. While many rivers continue to recede and \nhave fallen below minor flood and action stage, low lying areas \nare still saturated and some rivers and creeks still remain in \nminor to moderate flood stage. Locations further downstream will\nsee delayed impacts with some having only recently crested and \nothers yet to crest over the next 24 hours. The San Juan River \nthrough the Four Corners and towards Bluff and Mexican Hat still\nare expected to remain above flood stage through at least \nThursday. Please continue to heed any remaining road closures. \nVisit water.noaa.gov for the latest river forecasts in your \narea.\n\n&&\n\n.GJT WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\n\nCO...Winter Weather Advisory until 5 AM MST Thursday for COZ010.\nUT...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...TGJT\nAVIATION...TGJT\nHYDROLOGY...TGJT\n"}}